<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Seals Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Three Seals Code, Kotmai Tra Sam Duang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Seals Law is a collection of law texts compiled in 1805 on the orders of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> of Siam, most of them laws of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> era that had survived the destruction of Ayutthaya in 1767. After a divorce case made the king suspect that the laws had been altered, a commission of judges, scribes and officials revised them in 11 months, producing 27 laws in 41 volumes, each stamped with the seals of the three ministries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatthai">Mahatthai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalahom">Kalahom</a> and Phrakhlang. It remained the working law of Siam until modern codes partly replaced it from 1908. Under a 1978 Supreme Court ruling, its parts not since replaced are still in force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-seals-law.jpg" alt="Three Seals Law" width="330" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Librovore · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangkok_National_Museum_2024-06-01_(038).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> took pains to preserve the Thai texts that had survived the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, among them the royal chronicles and religious works, and in 1804 he completed a revision of the Tipiṭaka, the Buddhist canon. He then turned to the laws. A court had granted a divorce to a woman, Amdaeng Pom, although she had committed adultery, and her husband Bunsi petitioned that the judge had been biased. Every copy of the marriage law upheld her right, which led the king to suspect that the texts had been tampered with.</p><p>His preface ordered learned men to cleanse the royal decrees and laws in the palace library from the Thammasat onwards, make them agree with the Pali, arrange them in chapters and correct any departures from justice. The commission was made up of three judges, four royal scribes and four officials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Royal Teachers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The laws were written in the folding books known as samut thai khao. Some of the 27 texts hold several laws, 41 in all, and the prefaces date each law in the Chula Sakarat era, though many of these dates were plainly corrupted in copying. Three working copies were kept, in the Royal Bedchamber, the court of justice and the Palace Library, with a fourth made soon after as a backup. Through neglect the volumes were scattered, and a search in 1980 found 80 volumes of the original copies and 17 of the backup. A Penal Code of 1908 and a Civil and Commercial Code issued between 1923 and 1935 replaced much of it; the surviving parts include the Lak Inthaphat, the Tenets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1849 Mot Amatyakun and the American missionary Dan Beach Bradley printed an edition, but King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> had the books destroyed; one copy of the first volume survives in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. With King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>'s permission Bradley printed it in two volumes in 1862–3. The French legal scholar Robert Lingat prepared the edition of 1938–9 for Thammasat University from which all modern editions stem, and the Royal Institute published a facsimile edition in 2007 for the bicentenary. Yoneo Ishii published an English summary in 1986, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a> questioned the dates in the prefaces, and Tamara Loos traced the code's replacement by modern law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Three Seals Law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rama I had the surviving laws of Ayutthaya revised into the <b>Three Seals Law</b> of 1805 and sponsored a council of monks to revise the Buddhist canon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the sakdina system, which King Borommatrailokkanat fixed in 1454 and the <b>Three Seals Law</b> records, each rank carried a number, in name the rai of land its holder was entitled to: 5 for a slave, 20 for a commoner, 600 for a learned monk, 10,000 for a…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
